  "query_preview": "..A persistent line of showers/storms has been\nlingering from the Bellemont area to Gray Mountain since about 11\nPM MST. Outside of this line of thunderstorms, the rest of our CWA\nremains mostly quiet. This weekend is shaping up to be rather \nactive in terms of monsoon activity, so let's dive into the \ndetails. \n\nHigh pressure will shift eastward over the next couple of days,\nalong with a trough pushing down from the Pacific Northwest. This\nsynoptic pattern will allow for increased monsoon moisture across\nthe state. This moisture increase is shown in the elevated PWAT\nvalues. Many locations along and south of the Rim are showing\nvalues greater than an inch, with areas elsewhere between half and\ninch to an inch. Following the higher PWAT values and the Slight\nRisk of excessive rainfall from WPC, we've issued a Flood Watch\nfor Yavapai County, Gila County, and Oak Creek and Sycamore \nCanyons for 5 PM MST today through 5 AM MST Sunday. Be sure to\nhave a way to receive warnings, and never drive through a flooded\nroadway. Heavy rainfall will not be the only concern with activity\nthe next few days, but also hail, frequent lightning, and\ngusty/erratic winds.\n\nNot only are we looking to receive widespread activity throughout\nthe afternoon/evening hours, but convection is forecast to persist\novernight Saturday and Sunday as well. \n\nWe'll start to see a slight decrease in moisture Tuesday onward,\nbut there is uncertainty with how much our daily chances for\nshowers/storms will drop off. For now, the NBM shows most activity\nbecoming limited to the higher terrain, but things could slightly\nchange depending on how much moisture funnels up from Tropical\nStorm Karina and how that plays with a shortwave that looks to\npush across the Great Basin.\n\nModel guidance seems to think the potential tropical\nstorm behind Karina could be slightly more impactful to the\nSouthwest, but that's beyond our forecast period, so confidence is\nlow at this time.",
